name: Bitwarden server work session - audit the custom-permission contract before a rework
repo: bitwarden-server
contract_symbol: Permissions
contract_file: src/Core/AdminConsole/Models/Data/Permissions.cs
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md), cycle 1,
# attempt 2 on this repo. RE-QUESTION, routed back from a measured two-arm mini-bench.
# The anchor is UNCHANGED (laws.md ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR); the
# question and the gold group are what moved. The superseded yaml is kept as
# `bitwarden-server.yaml.conversion-boundary.bak`.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRAJECTORY - every previous cycle, and which side of the window it fell on
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The window is BOTH conditions at once: plain arm <= 0.50 AND ours >= plain + 0.50.
#
#   cycle 1, attempt 1  "the representation-boundary conversion audit"
#     verdict REQUESTION | baseline dependents 0.6842 (13/19) | sense 0.8421 (16/19)
#     delta +0.1579 | n = baseline x1, sense x1 (confirm_band: SETTLED, no second pair)
#     SIDE OF THE WINDOW: the BASELINE-TOO-HIGH side. It failed condition one by 0.18,
#     and that failure took condition two with it arithmetically - `1.00 - 0.6842 =
#     +0.3158` caps the delta below the +0.50 bar, so a perfect 19/19 sense arm still
#     lands 0.18 short. The cell was dead before it ran.
#
# THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM THE BASELINE-TOO-HIGH SIDE, and it intends to move the
# BASELINE down rather than the sense arm up. The sense arm was never the problem: it
# reached 16 of 19 in 183s of a 480s wall, on 3 tool calls, with `tool_use_audit`
# reporting no misuse flags and `transcript_miss.py` reporting `resolver-sev=0` and no
# miss rows. What has to move is the 0.6842.
#
# HOW FAR IT INTENDS TO MOVE, and the arithmetic under it. Scoring is per-FILE
# (`bench/lib/gold.py`: `_cited` matches a gold path pinned to ANY line), so re-pinning
# a file at a different line cannot change what an arm scores - only the FILE LIST can.
# On the measured pair, of the 12 files in the new group the baseline cited 4 and the
# sense arm cited 8 of the 10 that existed then (2 are new). That projects baseline
# 4/12 = 0.333 and sense 0.83-1.00, i.e. delta +0.50 to +0.67. THAT PROJECTION IS A
# RETRO-FIT ON n=1 PER ARM AND IS NOT A RESULT - the same n that `laws.md` says is too
# thin to re-gold from (`do not re-gold below ~5 runs per arm`). It is stated so the
# next phase can check the draft against what it was aiming at, and the mini-bench is
# what decides. Landing on the far side - baseline near zero because NEITHER arm can
# answer - is the failure mode this correction has to avoid, and the sense arm's
# measured 16/19 on ten of these twelve files is the only guard against it.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT KILLED THE PREVIOUS QUESTION - quoted from the run, not inferred
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `bench/lib/baseline_route.py` on the baseline transcript, 15 tool calls, all Bash,
# 9 of them searches. Call 2/3 produced the candidate file set outright:
#
#     grep -rln "Permissions" --include=*.cs src/ | head -60
#
# and call 11 closed it in one alternation:
#
#     grep -rn "new Permissions\|GetPermissions()\|SetPermissions(\|
#              LoadClassFromJsonData<Permissions>\|Permissions>(" --include="*.cs" ...
#
# The previous axis said a dependent "holds or produces its OWN copy of the same
# switches in another form, so the work it does is a conversion between two encodings".
# In this codebase that conversion is spelled with a closed, small set of LITERAL
# tokens, so describing the mechanism handed over the grep pattern. `LoadClassFromJsonData
# <Permissions>` alone was 6 of the 19 gold rows - six near-identical constructor lines,
# a free floor of 0.32 - and the `new Permissions` / `.ToData()` alternation was 5 more.
# Eleven of the baseline's thirteen rows came from those two strings.
#
# The six rows the baseline MISSED share a shape, and the credit table is where that is
# recorded: `d:acting-user` (a primary-constructor property), `d:base-org-profile` (a
# member on a shared base), `d:invite-data` (a plain property), `d:invite-business` (a
# by-reference hand-off), `d:org-user-request` (the encode direction call 11 omitted),
# `d:security-task-handler` (a nested property pattern). In every one the dependency is
# a DECLARATION-LEVEL fact - what the type declares, what it inherits, where in a
# constructor it sits - not a conversion the matched line states by itself.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AXIS SENTENCE - the mechanism this scenario's whole thesis rests on
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The rework changes the SHAPE of the set. What a shape change breaks is not the code
# that copies the set around - that code keeps compiling and is found by one search for
# the name. It is every OTHER type that has the set written into its own DECLARED SHAPE,
# so the set is part of what that type promises its own callers and clients. Five
# declaration-level mechanisms carry it here: a type declares the set as one of its own
# members; a type inherits that member from a base and never restates it; a type takes
# the set in a fixed position of its constructor; a type holds an ABSTRACTION whose
# implementation carries the set, so the set is inside it at one remove and the type's
# own text never names it; a type names a single switch inside a shape it matches
# against. A conversion site is not on this list, and that is deliberate - the eleven
# rows one alternation printed last cycle are conversion sites, and they are out of the
# scored group.
#
# INVENTORY CUT, applied by hand as laws.md THE ASK NAMES THE MECHANISM, NEVER THE
# INVENTORY requires. The five clauses in step 2 name HOW the set is held (declared
# member, inherited member, constructor position, abstraction held at one remove,
# matched shape). None of them names a functional area, a layer, a payload kind or a
# feature. `scenario.py --prompt` reads TOKENS and would pass a semantic inventory
# clean, so this cut is hand-applied or not at all.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEAM PROFILE - `python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Permissions --propose`, quoted
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   === Permissions  (lang=csharp, token='Permissions', hops=mcp-default, conf=mcp-default) ===
#     app caller files : 9   (total incl test/non-app: 38)
#     SCATTER (dirs)   : 9
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Permissions' in 540 app files   PRECISION=0.02  (lower=grep-hostile)
#     HOLDERS (no call): 15 app files compose/inherit/include it
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#
# PRECISION 0.02 (9 caller files in 540 grep files), SCATTER 9. Unchanged from attempt
# 1 - the anchor did not move. The profile RANKED this anchor first and it decided
# nothing: the two-arm run rejected the question, which is the division of labour
# `laws.md` PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER KILLS describes. Note for the record that 0.02
# did NOT predict the baseline's 0.6842; precision orders candidates, it does not
# forecast a cell.
#
# STACK FACT, carried forward from attempt 1 because it still holds: C# dependents HOLD
# an injected interface rather than calling it, and Sense carries that as a composition
# edge, so PRECISION is degenerate on every INTERFACE in this repo (`ICurrentContext`
# reads 0.00 with 0 direct callers and 108 composition rows, for a name one grep prints
# in 145 files). Read 0.00 on a C# interface as "no call edges exist", never as
# "grep-hostile". Comparable only within the concrete-class candidates.
#
# RETENTION RING - `python3 bench/lib/ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12`, quoted:
#   0 anchor(s) above the ranking threshold. Best rings found:
#   User rows=68 files=68 vias=1 | IUserService rows=16 files=16 vias=5 |
#   IOrganizationUserRepository rows=6 files=6 vias=4
# So the sweep's top-12 ranking is empty at its bar. For THIS anchor, however,
# `sense_blast Permissions` returns `retained_via_interfaces_count: 3`, and unlike
# `ICurrentContext` (where 87 retained rows are trimmed OUT with `retained_trimmed:
# true`) those three rows ARE shown in full:
#   AutomaticallyConfirmOrganizationUserRequest.cs:10  via IActingUser  chain StandardUser > Permissions
#   AutomaticallyConfirmOrganizationUserRequest.cs:25  via IActingUser  chain StandardUser > Permissions
#   PolicyUpdate.cs:13                                 via IActingUser  chain StandardUser > Permissions
# Two distinct FILES, and both are NEW to gold this cycle (`d:autoconfirm-request`,
# `d:policy-update`). They are the only rows in the group whose file never writes the
# anchor token at all. That is a property of these two rows, NOT the selection
# criterion for the group - laws.md TOKEN-DARKNESS IS NOT THE MECHANISM, and ten of the
# twelve rows below do write the token on their credit line.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANSWER FORM
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `verticals/csharp-aspnet/answer-forms.md` is still empty (every section reads "nothing
# measured yet") - the minibench verdict has not been folded into it yet, so this draft
# reads it as carrying nothing.
#
# The form attempt 1 used was "find every place that depends on X", an occurrence list.
# It is now MEASURED TO FAIL on this repo: baseline 0.6842, one `grep -rln` for the file
# set and one alternation for the idioms. This draft does NOT re-buy it.
#
# The form this draft uses is "what HOLDS X" - which types have the contract written
# into their own declared shape. It is UNMEASURED in this stack. It is the strongest
# measured kind elsewhere in the bench (+0.58, +0.67, +0.80, +1.00 on four Go cells),
# and `laws.md` gates it on a non-empty retention ring; the ring here is thin (2 files,
# quoted above) but non-empty and SHOWN, so the kind is on the menu rather than off it.
# It is being asked here in a wider sense than the pure retained-via surface - held
# members and inherited members count too - and that widening is what makes twelve rows
# possible on a two-file ring.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS: it is the set of
# types whose OWN published shape changes when the set is reshaped - each paired with
# which of the five declaration mechanisms puts the set inside it. A name-occurrence
# list gets this wrong in both directions at once. It OVER-shoots: the eleven conversion
# sites one alternation printed last cycle write the name and their own shape does not
# change, so they are not answers. It UNDER-shoots: two of the twelve types below never
# write the name anywhere in their file, because they hold an abstraction whose
# implementation carries it. There is no search over the name that separates those two
# errors, because the distinguishing fact - what a type DECLARES versus what it merely
# touches - is not on the matched line.
#
# MEMORIZATION: `memorization_probe.py $CLONE Permissions --json` returned
# `"applicable": false, "reason": "only 0 member(s) declared (< 5) - too few to score
# recitation, bar inapplicable"` - the class declares auto-properties and no methods, so
# the probe cannot score it. The probe is live and does discriminate: run on
# `CurrentContext` in the same clone it found 35 members and returned
# `"closed-book recall 0.0 < 0.3 - not recited"`. The clone is depth-1
# (`git rev-list --count HEAD` = 1) so churn-dating is unavailable; gold is scored at
# `path:line` in a 5306-file tree, the line-level structural fact the law exempts.
#
# Single-prompt session bench (bench/drivers/bench-sense-local.sh): every step is presented
# in ONE prompt and worked in order. Rendered to the agent: the description + each step's
# name and prompt ONLY (checks and gold are NEVER shown). Everything rendered is kept
# NEUTRAL - it states the task, never the answer's shape, file paths, class or method
# names, counts, or which tool to reach for.
description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework the way this product describes a fine-grained
  capability grant - the small fixed set of yes/no switches an organization can hand an
  individual member instead of a whole role. The rework will change the shape of that set:
  what a switch is called, how many there are, and how the set is written down. Plenty of
  code copies that set from one carrier to another, and most of that code will keep
  working. What will not is anything that has baked the set into its own shape, because
  then the set is part of what that thing promises to whoever uses it, and reshaping the
  set reshapes their contract too. Before you touch it you have to know two things: how
  the set is defined, stored and carried to the code that enforces it, and which other
  things in the codebase now carry the set as part of their own shape. Throughout, an
  answer that points at a place must say which type, member or routine that place is
  inside, and give an exact file:line a teammate could jump straight to, plus one phrase
  on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a file:line with nothing named
  around it does not count, and a missed one is a regression shipped.
steps:
- name: Map the custom-permission contract
  prompt: "Task: orient, and be thorough - the rest of the ticket is built on this. Find
    where the set of capability switches is defined, and then follow the one path the
    enforcement side leans on: how a member's granted switches are put away with that
    member's stored record, and how they are read back out and put in front of the code
    that has to decide whether a request is allowed. Show where the set is declared, where
    it is turned into the stored form and back again, and where it is rebuilt for the
    duration of a request from whatever the signed-in caller arrived carrying. For each one
    give the file:line, the name of the member or routine it lives in, and one phrase on
    what it contributes to that path. Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework would
    have to preserve for that path to keep working. A place named without the routine it
    lives in does not count, and neither does a routine named without the file:line where
    it is defined.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '3'
    required: true
    description: The declaration, the stored form and the per-request rebuild, each located
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_search
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the custom-permission contract
  prompt: "Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the
    first part already cost you. Widen back out from that one path and take the set of
    switches as a single subject. Code that merely copies the set from one carrier to
    another is NOT what you are after - that code goes on compiling, and it is not what a
    reshape breaks. What you owe is every other thing that has made this set part of its
    OWN shape, so that the set is now inside what it promises to whoever holds it or reads
    what it produces. It gets in there in several different ways, and each way looks
    different at the point of declaration: something can declare the set as one of its own
    members; it can inherit that member from something above it and never mention the set
    again itself; it can take the set in a fixed position of the thing that constructs it;
    it can hold on to an abstraction whose real implementation is what carries the set, so
    the set is inside it at one remove and its own text never says so anywhere; and it can
    name a single one of the switches inside a shape it tests a value against. Work out
    which things are in that position, by whichever of those routes. For each one, give the
    file:line of the declaration that puts the set inside its shape, name the thing that
    declaration belongs to, say which of those routes put it there, and say in one phrase
    what its own users would start getting wrong if the set were reshaped underneath it. A
    file:line with nothing named around it does not count, one you cannot state the
    consequence for does not count, and a missed one is a regression shipped.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Holders attributed to the declaration that carries the set, with the route named
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.70
    efficiency: 0.30
# Gold. TWO groups.
#   - `contract` (3, NON-SCORING anchors): the class that declares the switches, the
#     stored-row entity that serializes them into a text column, and the request-context
#     object that rebuilds them from token claims. These are step 1's targets. Both arms
#     reached all 3 on the measured pair and they do NOT score; they are here so step 1's
#     cost is charged without step 1 handing anything to step 2.
#   - `dependents` (12, THE DISCRIMINATOR): one row per FILE, across 11 distinct
#     directories in 4 top-level areas (public API models, internal API request/response
#     models, core admin-console models and features, and one authorization handler).
#     Every row is a DECLARATION that puts the set inside another type's shape. Not one
#     of them is a conversion call, and not one is on the declare/store/rebuild path
#     step 1 traces. Every row appears in the shown `sense_blast Permissions` payload at
#     MCP defaults - `direct_callers`, `affected_via_composition`, `affected_subclasses`
#     or `retained_via_interfaces` - and every line below was opened and read by hand at
#     its pin (the payload's own `ref` is the enclosing declaration, not the credit line,
#     for most of them).
#
# ROWS CUT FROM THE PREVIOUS GOLD, and the reason. Eight of attempt 1's nineteen
# dependents are gone. All eight are CONVERSION sites, so all eight fall outside this
# question's mechanism, and all eight were cited by the baseline in the measured pair:
#   d:org-profile, d:provider-profile, d:provider-user-response, d:ctx-organization,
#   d:ctx-provider
#     - the `LoadClassFromJsonData<Permissions>` block; one grep string, five
#       near-identical constructor lines among the baseline's 13 rows (the sixth,
#       `d:org-user-response`, is kept - see below).
#   d:org-service-invite, d:grant-validation, d:claims-extensions
#     - the `new Permissions` / `SetPermissions(` alternation and the second decoder.
# One file from the first block is KEPT: `d:org-user-response`, because under THIS
# question the file's contribution is not the decode at line 32 but the member declared
# at line 65 and inherited unrestated by the two subclasses at 71 and 139. It is kept
# knowing the baseline cited it.
# Every row the baseline MISSED last cycle is a holder under this mechanism, and every
# one of those six is kept.
#
# THE n UNDER THAT CUT, stated because it is thin. `inverse_frequency.py` over the only
# results root on disk reports `2 runs: baseline x1, sense x1`, and laws.md says the
# ordering is noise below ~5 runs per arm and forbids re-golding off it. So the cut was
# NOT made from that ranking. It was made from the MECHANISM - a conversion site is not
# a holder, and every cut row is a conversion site - and the citation record is quoted
# as corroboration, not as the reason.
#
# WHAT COULD STILL GO WRONG, named so the next phase reads the run and not this header:
# the two `IActingUser` rows are the only ones whose file never writes the token, and
# the sense arm has never been measured on them; if it does not surface
# `retained_via_interfaces`, the sense ceiling drops to 10/12 = 0.833 and the delta
# needs the baseline at or under 0.333 to clear the bar. That is the run's question.
gold:
- {id: contract-class, group: contract, match: [src/Core/AdminConsole/Models/Data/Permissions.cs], relation: "src/Core/AdminConsole/Models/Data/Permissions.cs:6 `public class Permissions` - the contract under rework; 13 boolean auto-properties, and line 23 `public List<(bool Permission, string ClaimName)> ClaimsMap => new()` is the one place the switches are paired with their claim names"}
- {id: c:stored-column, group: contract, match: [src/Core/AdminConsole/Entities/OrganizationUser.cs], relation: "src/Core/AdminConsole/Entities/OrganizationUser.cs:157 `Permissions = CoreHelpers.ClassToJsonData(permissions);` inside `OrganizationUser.SetPermissions`, with the read side at line 152 `: CoreHelpers.LoadClassFromJsonData<Permissions>(Permissions);` inside `GetPermissions` (declared line 149) - the obvious write path, where the bag becomes a text column on the stored member row"}
- {id: c:claims-rebuild, group: contract, match: [src/Core/Context/CurrentContext.cs], relation: "src/Core/Context/CurrentContext.cs:494 `return new Permissions` inside `CurrentContext.SetOrganizationPermissionsFromClaims` (declared line 486) - the per-request rebuild, 13 `hasClaim(\"...\")` lookups turning token claims back into the bag"}
- {id: d:base-org-profile, group: dependents, match: [src/Api/AdminConsole/Models/Response/BaseProfileOrganizationResponseModel.cs], relation: "src/Api/AdminConsole/Models/Response/BaseProfileOrganizationResponseModel.cs:138 `public Permissions? Permissions { get; set; }` - DECLARED MEMBER on the shared base of both sync payloads; this one declaration is what fixes the wire shape every client parses, and the two subclasses never restate it"}
- {id: d:provider-org-profile, group: dependents, match: [src/Api/AdminConsole/Models/Response/ProfileProviderOrganizationResponseModel.cs], relation: "src/Api/AdminConsole/Models/Response/ProfileProviderOrganizationResponseModel.cs:11 `public class ProfileProviderOrganizationResponseModel : BaseProfileOrganizationResponseModel` - INHERITED MEMBER; the class never declares the set, it gets the member from the base, and at line 21 fills it with an all-false bag so the empty default is this payload's behaviour"}
- {id: d:org-user-response, group: dependents, match: [src/Api/AdminConsole/Models/Response/Organizations/OrganizationUserResponseModel.cs], relation: "src/Api/AdminConsole/Models/Response/Organizations/OrganizationUserResponseModel.cs:65 `public Permissions Permissions { get; set; }` - DECLARED MEMBER on the admin-console member payload, inherited unrestated by `OrganizationUserDetailsResponseModel` (line 71) and `OrganizationUserUserDetailsResponseModel` (line 139) in the same file, so one declaration fixes three response shapes"}
- {id: d:org-user-request, group: dependents, match: [src/Api/AdminConsole/Models/Request/Organizations/OrganizationUserRequestModels.cs], relation: "src/Api/AdminConsole/Models/Request/Organizations/OrganizationUserRequestModels.cs:23 `public Permissions Permissions { get; set; }` on `OrganizationUserInviteRequestModel` - DECLARED MEMBER on the inbound invite body, declared a second time at line 102 on `OrganizationUserUpdateRequestModel`; both are the request shape a client has to send"}
- {id: d:public-permissions-model, group: dependents, match: [src/Api/AdminConsole/Public/Models/PermissionsModel.cs], relation: "src/Api/AdminConsole/Public/Models/PermissionsModel.cs:15 `public PermissionsModel(Permissions? data)` - CONSTRUCTOR POSITION; the public API declares its own parallel copy of all 13 switches as flat properties (lines 37-49) and this constructor is where the internal set is required to line up with it, so a reshape silently desynchronises the two lists"}
- {id: d:invite-data, group: dependents, match: [src/Core/AdminConsole/Models/Data/Organizations/OrganizationUsers/OrganizationUserInviteData.cs], relation: "src/Core/AdminConsole/Models/Data/Organizations/OrganizationUsers/OrganizationUserInviteData.cs:15 `public Permissions Permissions { get; set; }` - DECLARED MEMBER on the serialized invite payload, so the set is part of what an invite persists and replays later"}
- {id: d:invite-business, group: dependents, match: [src/Core/AdminConsole/Models/Business/OrganizationUserInvite.cs], relation: "src/Core/AdminConsole/Models/Business/OrganizationUserInvite.cs:14 `public Permissions Permissions { get; set; }` - DECLARED MEMBER on the core-layer invite, filled by reference at line 27 `Permissions = requestModel.Permissions;` inside the `OrganizationUserInvite(OrganizationUserInviteData)` constructor, so the API-layer shape and this one are the same object"}
- {id: d:invite-command-model, group: dependents, match: [src/Core/AdminConsole/OrganizationFeatures/OrganizationUsers/InviteUsers/Models/OrganizationUserInviteCommandModel.cs], relation: "src/Core/AdminConsole/OrganizationFeatures/OrganizationUsers/InviteUsers/Models/OrganizationUserInviteCommandModel.cs:15 `public Permissions Permissions { get; private init; }` - DECLARED MEMBER with private init, so it can only be set through the long-form constructor's CONSTRUCTOR POSITION at line 48 `Permissions permissions,` (constructor declared line 44); a reshape moves an argument here, not a property"}
- {id: d:acting-user, group: dependents, match: [src/Core/AdminConsole/Models/Data/StandardUser.cs], relation: "src/Core/AdminConsole/Models/Data/StandardUser.cs:12 `public Permissions? Permissions { get; } = permissions;` - DECLARED MEMBER fed from the primary-constructor parameter on line 7, on the one `IActingUser` implementation that carries the set; this declaration is the single reason the set reaches anything typed as the abstraction"}
- {id: d:autoconfirm-request, group: dependents, match: [src/Core/AdminConsole/OrganizationFeatures/OrganizationUsers/AutoConfirmUser/AutomaticallyConfirmOrganizationUserRequest.cs], relation: "src/Core/AdminConsole/OrganizationFeatures/OrganizationUsers/AutoConfirmUser/AutomaticallyConfirmOrganizationUserRequest.cs:16 `public required IActingUser PerformedBy { get; init; }` on `AutomaticallyConfirmOrganizationUserRequest`, with the same hold at line 31 on `AutomaticallyConfirmOrganizationUserValidationRequest` - ABSTRACTION HELD AT ONE REMOVE; the set is inside this request via `StandardUser`, and the file never writes the contract's name anywhere"}
- {id: d:policy-update, group: dependents, match: [src/Core/AdminConsole/OrganizationFeatures/Policies/Models/PolicyUpdate.cs], relation: "src/Core/AdminConsole/OrganizationFeatures/Policies/Models/PolicyUpdate.cs:21 `public IActingUser? PerformedBy { get; set; }` inside `PolicyUpdate` (declared line 13) - ABSTRACTION HELD AT ONE REMOVE; every policy save carries an actor whose implementation holds the set, and this file never writes the contract's name either"}
- {id: d:security-task-handler, group: dependents, match: [src/Core/Vault/Authorization/SecurityTasks/SecurityTaskOrganizationAuthorizationHandler.cs], relation: "src/Core/Vault/Authorization/SecurityTasks/SecurityTaskOrganizationAuthorizationHandler.cs:45 `{ Type: OrganizationUserType.Custom, Permissions.AccessReports: true };` inside `CanListAllTasksForOrganization` (declared line 41) - MATCHED SHAPE; a nested property pattern that names ONE switch through a field of the request-context organization, so a renamed switch fails to compile here and nowhere near the contract"}
